Sigh.

You are running into a situation that has existed
throughout the development of NetHack:

* The DevTeam adds some code.

* Some pathologically clever player finds a way to
  use that code that wasn't in the design specs for
  the code; not a bug, not even a misfeature, just
  purely clever play using a feature in an
  unanticipated way.

* Often that clever play involves great tedium that
  other players aren't willing to experience.

* Suddenly those other players feel like, by not
  adopting the cleverness, they are somehow being
  "cheated" by those who do, not bothering to think
  that the games they play are probably a lot more
  fun than the games the cleverness using players
  play.

* A great hue and cry washes across the NetHack
  online community: "this is unfair!", "I want my
  purchase price back!", "NetHack is for people
  who play it the way _I_ do!", usw.

* The DevTeam bows to the pressure, and patches out
  the prospect of exercising that cleverness.

* Lather, rinse, repeat.

I really miss digging two holes next to the corner
of a shop, piling stuff between them from within
the shop, going outside and kicking stuff out of the
shop without ever being in it myself. It took me
over a year to invent that trick.

Seems a lot of other people invented it too, and
they snitched.

Now the shop walls heal themselves.

Why? Not because stealing from shops isn't part of
the game, but because some envious persons hated
that others had thought up a ploy they themselves
had not imagined.

"Curses, foiled again!"

And we go back to robbing shops the slow, steady
way, hoping neither the PC nor the pet starves in
the process, while sniffing around the corners of
the game for another unnoticed code-consequence to
exploit.

Folks thinking this cycle will ever end are referred
to the discussions on ever escalating conflict
between the developers of the record player that can
play any record, versus the record that can shake
apart any record player, in _Goedel, Escher, Bach,
an Eternal Golden Braid_.

Meanwhile, I _like_ credit cloning; it has lots of
gnarly aspects, it has complicated ways to make it
work better, which I'm still inventing, lots of
things can go terribly wrong if the player is the
least bit careless, surviving it is about as
challenging as standing in the shop door calling
"here, kitty", and it leads to precisely the same
results as walking into the shop with a rabid pet
Archon: I get the money and the gold, the shopkeeper
gets shafted. Only difference is, the shopkeeper
doesn't have to *die* in the credit cloning version,
which appeals to my ethics: only kill enemies.

[Now if you want to get rid of a _real_ abuse, make
it impossible to magic-whistle a pet out of a shop.]

